ght: 800; color: var(--allo-stem-text, #e2e8f0); margin-bottom: 8px;"},"A note on this analysis"],["DIV",{"style":"font-size: 12px; color: var(--allo-stem-text, #cbd5e1); line-height: 1.65;"},["P",{"style":"margin: 0px 0px 10px;"},"Model scope: one idealized truss plane with pin joints and solid square members. Quantities cover that plane only; the second truss, deck, lateral bracing, connections and labor are excluded. Only the entered loads are applied; self-weight is not added automatically. All materials use a single nominal strength in both tension and compression, which cannot represent brittle stone, cast iron, grain direction or reinforced-concrete behavior."],["SPAN",{},["STRONG",{"style":"color: rgb(134, 239, 172);"},"✓ Exact analysis (Method of Joints). "],"Forces are computed from 2D pin-jointed equilibrium: for each joint, sum of forces in x and sum of forces in y both equal zero. ","The resulting linear system (18 equations, 18 unknowns) is solved by Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting. Hover over any member in the diagram to see its exact tension or compression force. The maximum chord force (125 kN) and maximum diagonal force (118 kN) are now the actual peak member forces, not deep-beam approximations."]]]]]]],[[4,20]],[[4,21]],[[4,22]],[[4,23]],[[4,24]],[[4,25]],[[4,26]],[[4,27]]]],"viewport":{"width":1280,"height":720},"timestamp":28501.505,"wallTime":1790653919987,"collectionTime":27.300000000046566,"resourceOverrides":[],"isMainFrame":true}}
