{"type":"after","callId":"call@164","endTime":29173.191,"result":{"value":{"s":"🌉\nBridge Engineering Lab\nEngineering Design · Structural Mechanics · NGSS HS-ETS1 + HS-PS2\nDESIGN BRIEF\nStress Test\n\nStart by stress-testing a bridge, then use material and force references only when you need evidence for a redesign.\n\nStress test\nChange span, loads, and truss style.\nMaterials\nCompare strength, cost, and density.\nForces\nRead tension, compression, shear, and torsion.\nDesign cycle\nPlan, test, revise, and document.\nDESIGN READINESS\nRedesign needed\nSF 0.58\nWarren truss, 4 bays, Structural Steel\nFAILED\nSPAN\n30 m\nLOAD\n50 kN\nMATERIAL\nStructural Steel\nSTRESS\n25 MPa\nMASS\n4282 kg\nMATERIAL COST\n$25694\nShow reference tabs\n🔨 Stress Test\n🌉 Bridge Types\n🧱 Materials\n⚖️ Forces\n🔁 Design Cycle\n📝 Quiz\n🧭 Start here\nRead the verdict card below the picture: is this bridge SAFE, MARGINAL, or FAILED, and why?\nDrag any slider and watch the verdict, the colors, and the numbers respond instantly. Breaking it on purpose is a great way to learn.\nSave your starting design in the notebook. Improve its safety margin, then compare material cost under the same applied load.\n\nIn every picture: red pieces are being STRETCHED (tension), blue pieces are being SQUEEZED (compression), and thicker means working harder.\n\nGot it — hide this\n\nWarren truss bridge stress test. Adjust the parameters below and see how force distribution changes. Red = tension, blue = compression, thickness shows magnitude. Forces are solved exactly by the method of joints — every member force satisfies equilibrium at both of its ends.\n\n3D structure\nOn the bridge\nLabelled 2D view\nReset view\nEarthquake experiment\n\nMove the ground, ride the deck, and follow how motion stores and dissipates energy.\n\n⛶\nDrag or arrow keys — orbit · Scroll or +/− — zoom\n\nThe same analysis is reported as text below: span, member forces, safety factor and buckling margin. Lateral bracing spacing is adjustable with the \"Lateral bracing\" slider in the Loading section.\n\nBuckling deformation is exaggerated to show the mode; it is not a displacement prediction.\n\nTension\nCompression\nThicker = larger force\nSupports: pin (left) ▲ · roller (right) ▲⊙\nLoading\nUniform load\ndistributed across all top joints (dead load equivalent)\nMoving vehicle\nsingle point load you can drag across the span\nUniform mode applies the entered load at every top joint. Total applied load changes when the number of top joints changes. Member self-weight is not added automatically.\nSpan (m)\n30\nThe distance between the two supports. Longer spans are MUCH harder: forces grow fast with span.\nHeight (m)\n6\nA taller truss spreads the load better, like a wider stance. Watch the safety factor rise as you raise it.\nBays (triangle sections)\n4\nHow many triangles the truss is divided into. More bays = shorter, sturdier pieces, but more joints to build.\nLoad per joint (kN)\n50\nThe weight pressing down at each top joint. Imagine trucks parked all along the deck.\nMember cross-section (mm²)\n5000\nLateral bracing: every N panels (1 = every joint)\n1\nCross-ties that stop the top chord flexing sideways. Fewer braces = longer free lengths = easier to buckle. This slider decides most verdicts here.\nTruss style\nWarren\nalternating diagonals, no verticals\nPratt\nverticals + inward diagonals (in tension)\nHowe\nverticals + outward diagonals (in compression)\nK-truss\nK-shaped pattern, shorter diagonals\nWarren trusses (1848, James Warren) use equilateral triangles with no verticals. Alternating diagonals carry shear in alternating tension/compression. Efficient and elegant; common in shorter spans.\nMaterial\nWood (softwood)\nStone\nCast Iron\nStructural Steel\nReinforced Concrete\nFRP Composite (fiber-reinforced polymer)\n✗ FAILED — at least one failure mode reached\nA long, thin piece would snap SIDEWAYS before the material itself ever failed. That is buckling, and it happens suddenly, with no warning creak.\n✓ Strength (yield)\nSafety factor 14.00 (max stress 25 MPa vs nominal strength 350 MPa)\n✗ Stability (buckling)\nGoverning member TC1: P_cr 73 kN vs compression 125 kN; length 7.5 m; slenderness 367 (pinned ends K=1; solid square section)\nBUCKLING FAILURE: member TC1 reaches its Euler buckling load before material yield. Long, thin compression members can buckle far below crushing strength. Thicken the section, shorten the unbraced length, add bracing, or revise the geometry.\nMAX CHORD FORCE\n125 kN\nThe biggest pull or squeeze in the long top/bottom pieces. (1 kN is about the weight of a washing machine.)\nMAX DIAGONAL FORCE\n118 kN\nThe biggest force in the zig-zag pieces. Diagonals work hardest near the supports.\nMAX STRESS\n25 MPa\nForce squeezed into each bit of material. Must stay well BELOW the yield number beside it.\nMATERIAL YIELD\n350 MPa\nThe stress where this material starts to permanently bend or tear. Your ceiling.\nBUCKLING LOAD (P_CR)\n73 kN\nThe push at which the weakest strut snaps sideways, like a spaghetti noodle pressed from both ends.\nWEAKEST STRUT\nTC1\nThe piece closest to buckling. Find it in the picture above; the whole bridge is only as safe as this one piece.\nSLENDERNESS RATIO (L/R)\n367\nLength divided by thickness. Long and thin = wobbly. Real design codes stop at about 200.\nPUSH-BACK AT EACH SUPPORT\n100 kN\nHow hard each end pushes back up. The two ends together carry the whole load.\nDIAGONAL ANGLE\n58°\nChanging the angle changes both the member length and force. Compare designs under the same load to see the trade-off.\nTOTAL MEMBER LENGTH\n109.1 m\nAll the pieces laid end to end. More length = more material = more cost.\nESTIMATED MASS\n4282 kg\nWhat the bare structure weighs. A bridge must carry itself before it carries anything else.\nESTIMATED MATERIAL COST\n$25694\nThe engineering game: the CHEAPEST design that is still SAFE wins.\n📖 The words on this page, in plain language\nFollow the load\n\nChoose a member to inspect its force and margin. In the labelled 2D view, its identifier marks the same member. Positive forces pull; negative forces compress.\n\nInspect member\nBC0 · B0 → B1\nBC1 · B1 → B2\nBC2 · B2 → B3\nBC3 · B3 → B4\nTC0 · T0 → T1\nTC1 · T1 → T2\nTC2 · T2 → T3\nDL0 · B0 → T0\nDR0 · T0 → B1\nDL1 · B1 → T1\nDR1 · T1 → B2\nDL2 · B2 → T2\nDR2 · T2 → B3\nDL3 · B3 → T3\nDR3 · T3 → B4\nTC1 · Compression · -125.00 kN\nMember length: 7.50 m · Stress: 25.00 MPa\nGoverning safety factor: 0.58\nBuckling length: 7.50 m\n\nSupport balance: 100.00 + 100.00 = 200.00 kN total applied load.\n\nAll member forces\nDesign notebook\n\nPredict what will happen, change one variable, then record what you observed. Save up to four trials with the design, your explanation, and any completed crossing test.\n\nPrediction — before the change\nObservation — after the test\nSave current design\n0 / 4\nOpen design report\n\nNo trials yet. Save this design before your next change.\n\nDesign notes (optional, included on print)\n💰 Cost optimization — what's the cheapest design that passes?\n\nCompare wood, structural steel and FRP sections against the chosen strength and buckling margin. Geometry and loads use your current settings. Stone, cast iron and reinforced concrete are excluded because this simple axial model does not represent their tensile or reinforcement behavior.\n\nResults cover the current uniform load at every loaded joint.\n\nTarget safety factor\n2.0\n1.5 (very thin margin)\n2.0 (learning target)\n4.0+ (conservative)\n6.0 (Brooklyn Bridge)\n✓ 61 combinations pass. Top 5 by cost:\nRank\tMaterial\tCross-section\tSF yield\tSF buckling\tMass\tCost (USD)\n🏆 1\tStructural Steel\t9,500 mm²\t26.60\t2.11\t8136 kg\t$48819\n2\tStructural Steel\t10,000 mm²\t28.00\t2.34\t8565 kg\t$51388\n3\tStructural Steel\t10,500 mm²\t29.40\t2.58\t8993 kg\t$53957\n4\tFRP Composite (fiber-reinforced polymer)\t21,000 mm²\t100.80\t2.06\t4582 kg\t$54988\n5\tFRP Composite (fiber-reinforced polymer)\t21,500 mm²\t103.20\t2.16\t4691 kg\t$56298\nNote: Cost estimate is material-only — doesn't include labor, fabrication, transport, fastening, or maintenance over the bridge's lifetime. Real bridge economics also factor in durability (steel maintenance vs concrete) + replacement cycle. But raw material cost is the starting line.\nClick the winner: Apply this design to my bridge\nA note on this analysis\n\nModel scope: one idealized truss plane with pin joints and solid square members. Quantities cover that plane only; the second truss, deck, lateral bracing, connections and labor are excluded. Only the entered loads are applied; self-weight is not added automatically. All materials use a single nominal strength in both tension and compression, which cannot represent brittle stone, cast iron, grain direction or reinforced-concrete behavior.\n\n✓ Exact analysis (Method of Joints). Forces are computed from 2D pin-jointed equilibrium: for each joint, sum of forces in x and sum of forces in y both equal zero. The resulting linear system (18 equations, 18 unknowns) is solved by Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting. Hover over any member in the diagram to see its exact tension or compression force. The maximum chord force (125 kN) and maximum diagonal force (118 kN) are now the actual peak member forces, not deep-beam approximations."}},"afterSnapshot":"after@call@164"}
